{{#*inline "page"}}
<div class="blog">
    <h1 class="blog_header"><span class="gradient-text">The shortest URL shorteners in 2026, measured</span></h1>

    <p>Many link shorteners call themselves the shortest. We shortened the same address with each of them and counted
        the characters of the link you get back, as it appears on screen, without <code>https://</code>.</p>

    <div class="blog-scroll">
        <table class="compare">
            <tr>
                <th>Service</th>
                <th>Link</th>
                <th class="num">Code</th>
                <th class="num">Total</th>
            </tr>
            <tr class="us">
                <td>pinch.to</td>
                <td><code>🤏.to/ꙮ𐐀</code></td>
                <td class="num">2</td>
                <td class="num">7</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>T.LY</td>
                <td><code>t.ly/abcd</code>*</td>
                <td class="num">4</td>
                <td class="num">9</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>da.gd</td>
                <td><code>da.gd/Vr0D1</code></td>
                <td class="num">5</td>
                <td class="num">11</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>v.gd</td>
                <td><code>v.gd/lcmljb</code></td>
                <td class="num">6</td>
                <td class="num">11</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>is.gd</td>
                <td><code>is.gd/AG3Hwv</code></td>
                <td class="num">6</td>
                <td class="num">12</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Bitly</td>
                <td><code>bit.ly/3xYz9Ab</code>*</td>
                <td class="num">7</td>
                <td class="num">14</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>clck.ru</td>
                <td><code>clck.ru/3WT4dG</code></td>
                <td class="num">6</td>
                <td class="num">14</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>X (t.co)</td>
                <td><code>t.co/AbCdEfGhIj</code>*</td>
                <td class="num">10</td>
                <td class="num">15</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>TinyURL</td>
                <td><code>tinyurl.com/2y5nzyk7</code></td>
                <td class="num">8</td>
                <td class="num">20</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Checked on 12 October 2026 by shortening a new address on each service. * Not made by us: the
        format the service itself shows. X shortens every link posted there, and only there.</p>

    <h2>Why the domains are not the difference</h2>
    <p>The shortest domain anyone can register has four characters: one letter, a dot, and a two-letter country
        ending, like <code>t.co</code>, <code>t.ly</code> or <code>🤏.to</code>. Every serious contender has one, so the
        race is decided by the code after the slash.</p>

    <h2>Why the codes are</h2>
    <p>Other shorteners build codes from the 62 letters and digits of the Latin alphabet. To tell billions of links
        apart with 62 symbols, a code needs five to seven of them. pinch.to draws from about 131,000 letters across all
        of Unicode's scripts, so two characters are enough for 17 billion different links.</p>

    <h2>The catch</h2>
    <p>Short to look at is not short in every sense. Browsers send <code>🤏.to/ꙮ𐐀</code> as
        <code>xn--rp9h.to/%EA%99%AE%F0%90%90%80</code>, and nobody can type <code>ꙮ</code> from memory. pinch.to links
        are made for reading, pasting and printing. If you need a link people can type, Bitly or T.LY with a
        custom name is the better choice.</p>

    <p><a href="/">Make a pinch.to link</a> · <a href="/blog/about">How pinch.to works</a></p>
